  "datasheet": "https://docs.arduino.cc/resources/datasheets/A000005-datasheet.pdf",
  "verified": true,
  "summary": "Breadboard-friendly 5 V Arduino: ATmega328P at 16 MHz, 14 digital I/O (6 PWM), 8 analog inputs (A6/A7 analog-only), FT232RL USB-serial on Mini-B.",
  "warnings": [
    {
      "level": "info",
      "text": "All I/O on this board is native 5 V logic: the ATmega328P runs from 5 V, so its pins output 5 V and accept 0 to 5 V (absolute max VCC + 0.5 V). They are not \"5 V tolerant\" pins, they simply are 5 V pins. Connecting a 3.3 V-only device directly to an output pin can damage it; use a level shifter."
    },
    {
      "level": "warn",
      "text": "D0 (RX) and D1 (TX) are shared with the FT232RL USB-serial chip. Anything connected to them can block sketch uploads and Serial Monitor.",
      "pins": [
        "J2-1",
        "J2-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "warn",
      "text": "A6 and A7 are analog inputs only: no digitalRead(), digitalWrite() or pinMode(), and no pull-up.",
      "pins": [
        "J1-10",
        "J1-11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "text": "A4 and A5 are the I2C pins SDA and SCL.",
      "pins": [
        "J1-8",
        "J1-9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "text": "Hardware SPI is on D10 (SS), D11 (MOSI), D12 (MISO) and D13 (SCK), and on the 6-pin ICSP pads.",
      "pins": [
        "J2-13",
        "J2-14",
        "J2-15",
        "J1-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "warn",
      "text": "Current limits: the ATmega328P absolute maximum is 40 mA DC per I/O pin and 200 mA through the VCC and GND pins (Microchip DS40002061B, Absolute Maximum Ratings). The datasheet specifies output levels only up to its 20 mA test condition, and Arduino's official pinout gives 20 mA as the maximum per I/O pin, so design for 20 mA or less per pin. The datasheet also limits the summed current of each port group (see its DC characteristics notes)."
    }
  ],
